      "instructionsEn": "Write 2-3 English sentences about yourself: something you used to be able to do, something you would like to be able to do, and something you have never been able to do.",
      "ruleSummary": "This unit practises can, could and (be) able to. can + infinitive covers possibility, permission and ability (We can see the lake; You can use mine), with the negative can't. can has only two forms, can and could, so (be) able to fills every other slot: I haven't been able to sleep, he might be able to come, you must be able to speak two languages. could is the past of can for general ability and with see/hear/smell/taste/feel/remember/understand (My grandfather could speak five languages; I could smell gas). But for succeeding in ONE specific past situation, English uses was/were able to or managed to, not could (Everybody was able to escape, not could escape). The negative couldn't works in every case. A correct student sentence must not use could for a single completed past achievement, must use be able to wherever can has no form, and must keep the base verb after can/could."
