      "ruleSummary": "This unit practises may and might for possibility. may and might are interchangeable here and mean perhaps (It may be true / It might be true; She might know), with negatives may not and might not. They are followed by a bare infinitive: be, be -ing, or an ordinary verb. For the past the forms are may/might (not) have + past participle (She may have been asleep; You might have left it at work; She might not have known about it). Keep may be (modal + verb) apart from maybe (one word, an adverb meaning perhaps). could is close in meaning to may and might for the positive (It could be true), but couldn't is NOT the negative of might not: couldn't have means it is not possible that it happened, while might not have means perhaps it did not. A correct student sentence must use a bare infinitive after may/might, may/might have + past participle for the past, and must express uncertainty rather than a firm conclusion."
