      "ruleSummary": "This unit practises have to and must. have to means it is necessary or I am obliged, and it behaves like an ordinary verb: it takes do/does/did in questions and negatives (Do you have to work? Karen doesn't have to work Saturdays; Did you have to wait?) and has all the forms must lacks (I'll have to, I might have to, I've had to). must and have to are both fine when the speaker gives an opinion or a recommendation (I must phone her; You must meet him), but a fact about somebody's obligation takes have to (I have to work from 8.30 to 5.30), while written rules take must (Seat belts must be worn). For the past only had to is possible, never must. mustn't and don't have to are opposites, not variants: You mustn't tell anyone means do not do it, while You don't have to come means it is not necessary. have got to can replace have to. A correct student sentence must not use must for the past or for a bare fact of obligation, and must not confuse mustn't with don't have to."
