      "ruleSummary": "This unit practises must, mustn't and needn't. You must do something means it is necessary; You mustn't do something means do not do it. needn't (and don't/doesn't need to) means it is not necessary, but you may if you want, so needn't is the opposite of must, while mustn't is a prohibition: You needn't tell Steve versus You mustn't tell Steve. Note the form: needn't do (no to) but don't need to do. needn't have + past participle says the person DID something that turned out to be unnecessary (We needn't have reserved a table, the restaurant was empty), whereas didn't need to do says it was not necessary, leaving open whether it was done (He didn't need to get up early, so he didn't). didn't have to can replace didn't need to. A correct student sentence must keep mustn't and needn't apart, must drop to after needn't, and must use needn't have + past participle only for something that was actually done."
